<reading-list id="reading" title="My reading">
  <@item>Every author states an order; none enforces it in code. All six are recommendations in a README, a guide or the skill text. Three hedge: omo defaults to the bare keyword and offers the planned path as opt-in, AI Hero makes its flow conditional and rejects tools that own the process, Compound Engineering publishes four orders that disagree. pstack has no single order, only one per playbook.</@item>
  <@item>What code does enforce is narrow, and it is never the order or the quality of a step. omo: a hook re-injects a continue prompt while the plan has unchecked boxes (native stops after 8 in a row, OpenCode after 3 rounds without a tool call, Codex has no counter), the planner agent can write only markdown under .omo, plan reviewers spawn only inside /ulw-plan, and on Codex a worker needs an evidence receipt to stop. Superpowers: one script refuses to record an inline task whose tests fail. Lavra: two hooks block a stop. Compound Engineering: a controller and a handshake script guard the optional cross-model path. pstack: watch-pr reports a PR state by exit code and blocks nothing. AI Hero: nothing. Every verdict, round limit and gate drawn on this page is otherwise a sentence the model is asked to follow.</@item>
  <@item>Current state is a gated step in two of six: omo (cited findings and gap rows) and pstack (mechanism confirmed at runtime before design). The other four research inside brainstorming or planning and write no current-state section to check. This is the gap the proposed Baseline step fills.</@item>
  <@item>A final check against the requirements exists in two of six: omo (final row F4 against the ideal-state rows) and Compound Engineering (review traces requirement IDs, blocking only when the plan was passed explicitly; the Definition of Done is re-checked by the implementer, not a reviewer). Both depend on requirements that carry IDs. Lavra and AI Hero stop at per-task checks; the Superpowers final reviewer asks only whether all planned functionality is present; pstack checks a done sentence.</@item>
  <@item>omo is the closest to the proposed Define and Baseline: ideal-state rows, gap rows, every todo closes a gap, every ideal-state row is proven at the end. It has no design artifact.</@item>
  <@item>No stack runs its loop at more than one level. Lavra (epic, beads) and Compound Engineering (plan, units) have two fixed levels. The recursion in the hyper proposal has no precedent here: not tested, not refuted.</@item>
  <@item>Five of six have a learning step and none makes it mandatory: the Lavra hook asks and never checks, Compound runs only when a belief was corrected, pstack reflect and the AI Hero retro are separate requests, omo memory is written on compaction or by step count. The hyper proposal has no such step. The strongest forms prefer a check to a note: the Compound Engineering ladder and pstack correct.</@item>
  <@item>Most specified fix loops are bounded: Superpowers 5 rounds then the controller rules, Lavra 3 then it reports and proceeds, omo native 3 attempts or a repeated failure. The omo OpenCode edition has no retry cap. No program counts any of these rounds; the only counters in code are the omo continuation limits and the Codex evidence gate. The proposed Replan has no bound.</@item>
  <@item>pstack and omo accept a verdict only from an agent that did not write the code (in omo that is a verifier subagent in the native edition and the orchestrator in the OpenCode edition). The proposal gives Verify to the author of the Define, which is the same rule.</@item>
</reading-list>

